Package: biokids
Architecture: all
# Its own description calls it a metapackage, and it ships no files at all, so
# it belongs in the section reserved for them rather than inheriting `admin`
# from the source stanza. dev/check_packaging.py uses this to tell a package
# that ships nothing on purpose from one whose .install file was forgotten.
Section: metapackages
Depends: biokids-core (= ${binary:Version}),
         biokids-daemon (= ${binary:Version}),
         biokids-panel (= ${binary:Version}),
         biokids-shell (= ${binary:Version}),
         biokids-voice (= ${binary:Version}),
         ${misc:Depends}
Recommends: biokids-archive-keyring
Suggests: biokids-ai, biokids-desktop
Description: parental controls for Debian that stay on your own machine
 BioKids is a metapackage that installs a complete, working set of parental
 controls on an existing Debian system: the enforcement daemon, the parent
 panel, the child launcher and the two voice companions, Zio and Sio.
 .
 What a parent gets is a daily time limit that a child cannot win back by
 moving the system clock, separate weekday and weekend rules, time windows,
 spoken warnings before the computer shuts down, and a launcher with big
 tiles instead of a desktop full of things a six-year-old should not find.
 .
 Nothing leaves the computer. There is no account to create, no telemetry, no
 analytics and no cloud service in the path. The parent panel listens on
 127.0.0.1 and is reached from a phone over the family's own private network.
 .
 BioKids is built for a curious child aged 4 to 10 working alone at one
 computer. It does not try to stop someone who boots from a USB stick, edits
 the kernel command line, takes the disk out, or has an adult helping them,
 and it protects one machine rather than a household. It is a supervision
 aid, not a guarantee, and it is no substitute for talking to your child.

Package: biokids-core
Architecture: all
Depends: adduser,
         alembic (>= 1.13),
         python3-argon2,
         python3-cryptography,
         python3-sqlalchemy (>= 2.0),
         ${misc:Depends},
         ${python3:Depends}
Recommends: biokids-daemon
Suggests: biokids-ai
Description: parental controls for Debian - shared library and data
 This package holds the parts every other BioKids package is built on: the
 loader for /etc/biokids/config.toml, the SQLite schema and its migrations,
 the policy engine, the Turkish and English message catalogues, and the
 application catalogue in /usr/share/biokids/catalog.
 .
 It also owns the family's data directory, /var/lib/biokids, and creates it
 on installation. Removing this package leaves that directory alone; only a
 purge offers to delete it, and it asks first.
 .
 On its own it does nothing a parent can see. Install the biokids package for
 a working system.
 .
 BioKids is built for a curious child aged 4 to 10 working alone at one
 computer. It does not try to stop someone who boots from a USB stick, edits
 the kernel command line or has an adult helping them.

Package: biokids-daemon
Architecture: all
Depends: biokids-core (= ${binary:Version}),
         dnsmasq-base,
         nftables,
         python3-dbus-next,
         systemd,
         ${misc:Depends},
         ${python3:Depends}
Recommends: biokids-panel
Suggests: biokids-ai
Description: parental controls for Debian - enforcement daemon
 The daemon is the part that actually counts a child's time and acts on it.
 It follows every login session through systemd-logind, accounts time against
 CLOCK_BOOTTIME so that winding the clock forward buys nothing, evaluates the
 policy the parent set, and warns, locks or shuts the machine down when the
 daily allowance runs out.
 .
 It runs as root, confined by a systemd sandbox, and speaks to the rest of
 BioKids over a Unix socket at /run/biokids/daemon.sock. If it dies, the
 child's session is locked rather than left unsupervised: a parental control
 that fails open is worse than none at all.
 .
 This package also brings in dnsmasq and nftables, which the filtering work
 in a later release configures. Neither is touched until a parent turns
 filtering on.
 .
 BioKids is built for a curious child aged 4 to 10 working alone at one
 computer. It does not defend against a booted USB stick, an edited kernel
 command line or an adult who is helping.

Package: biokids-panel
Architecture: all
Depends: biokids-core (= ${binary:Version}),
         biokids-daemon (= ${binary:Version}),
         python3-fastapi,
         python3-itsdangerous,
         python3-jinja2,
         python3-python-multipart,
         python3-qrcode,
         python3-uvicorn,
         ${misc:Depends},
         ${python3:Depends}
# The menu entry and the first-login wizard open the panel with xdg-open
# (D-170). Every desktop has it; a server install without one has no menu.
Recommends: xdg-utils
Suggests: biokids-ai
Description: parental controls for Debian - parent panel
 The panel is how a parent sets everything up and sees what happened today:
 a setup wizard, a daily quota per child, time windows, the audit log, and
 three buttons that reach the daemon directly - grant fifteen minutes, lock
 the session now, and shut the computer down.
 .
 It listens on 127.0.0.1:7878 only. There is no port to forward and no
 account to create; reaching it from a phone is done over the family's own
 private network. Access is gated by a six-digit PIN hashed with argon2id and
 rate limited, with a separate passphrase for anything destructive.
 .
 The interface is in Turkish and English, and it is written for a parent who
 does not want to learn what a systemd unit is.
 .
 BioKids is built for a curious child aged 4 to 10 working alone at one
 computer, and the panel says so where a parent will read it.

