Recommends: biokids-voice
Suggests: biokids-desktop
Description: parental controls for Debian - child launcher
 Instead of a desktop, a child gets a full-screen grid of large tiles holding
 exactly the applications the parent picked, a ring showing how much time is
 left today, and nothing else: no file manager, no settings, no terminal, no
 way to minimise it.
 .
 The launcher is a small local web application shown in a Chromium kiosk
 window, started as a systemd user unit inside the child's own session. It
 has no privileges of its own; every decision about what may run and for how
 long comes from the daemon.
 .
 The tile grid changes with the child's age: the four-to-seven layout is
 larger, has fewer tiles and uses pictures rather than words.
 .
 Over every app the child opens, the same four big buttons sit in the
 screen's top-right corner: back to the launcher, the app's own volume down
 and up, and close - which stops an app that will not close by itself.
 .
 BioKids is built for a curious child aged 4 to 10 working alone at one
 computer. The launcher cannot be left with a keyboard or a mouse, which is
 not the same thing as being unescapable.

Package: biokids-voice
Architecture: all
Section: sound
Depends: biokids-core (= ${binary:Version}),
         espeak-ng,
         pipewire,
         ${misc:Depends},
         ${python3:Depends}
Suggests: mbrola-tr1, mbrola-tr2
Description: parental controls for Debian - Zio and Sio, the voice companions
 Zio and Sio are the two characters who tell a child what is happening: ten
 minutes left, five minutes left, one minute left, time to stop. A spoken
 warning lands with a four-year-old in a way that a notification never does,
 and what they say always appears as text on screen as well, so a child who
 is not listening still sees it.
 .
 Speech is produced on the machine itself. Nothing is recorded, nothing is
 uploaded and no microphone is ever opened. espeak-ng is what speaks by
 default: it is in Debian, it needs no model file and no download, and it
 speaks Turkish. It also sounds like a 1990s robot, which is the honest trade
 against a child being shown a card they may not be able to read yet.
 .
 Piper sounds far better and is used instead whenever it is installed, but it
 is not in Debian and its remaining Turkish voice may not be redistributed, so
 it cannot be the default. A parent can download both with one button in the
 parent panel, from their authors, for the family's own non-commercial use;
 every file is checked against a pinned checksum, and nothing else in this
 package uses the network. Where mbrola-tr1 and mbrola-tr2 are installed,
 Zio and Sio speak with those recorded Turkish voices instead, which sounds far
 more like a person. They are suggested rather than recommended because the
 voices are in non-free and mbrola itself in contrib.
 .
 The service runs inside the child's session with no privileges, and ducks
 whatever else is playing to thirty percent while it speaks.

Package: biokids-desktop
Architecture: all
Section: metapackages
Depends: biokids (= ${binary:Version}),
         chromium,
         lightdm,
         xfce4,
         ${misc:Depends}
Recommends: frozen-bubble,
            gcompris-qt,
            gnome-mahjongg,
            klavaro,
            kolourpaint,
            supertux,
            tuxmath,
            tuxpaint
Suggests: biokids-ai, libreoffice-writer
Description: parental controls for Debian - full child desktop (metapackage)
 This metapackage turns a plain Debian installation into the machine a child
 is handed: BioKids itself, the Xfce session it is built on, the LightDM
 greeter with its large avatars, and the applications in the BioKids
 catalogue - GCompris, TuxPaint, TuxMath, Klavaro, KolourPaint,
 SuperTux, Frozen Bubble and gnome-mahjongg.
 .
 The applications arrive as recommendations, so a parent who wants a smaller
 set can drop any of them without fighting the package manager. Nothing a
 parent has not enabled in the panel is ever shown to the child, whether it
 is installed or not.
 .
 Install this on a machine that is going to be a child's computer. On a
 shared machine, install the biokids package instead and leave the parent's
 own desktop as it is.

Package: biokids-ai
Architecture: all
Depends: biokids-core (= ${binary:Version}),
         llama.cpp-tools (>= 0.5.0),
         ${misc:Depends},
         ${python3:Depends}
Recommends: kiwix-tools, whisper.cpp-tools
Enhances: biokids-daemon, biokids-shell
Description: parental controls for Debian - optional local assistant
 An optional assistant in the child's launcher: the child types or says a
 question - why is the sky blue? - and gets a short answer pitched at their
 age, on screen and read aloud. It is off until a parent turns it on for a
 child.
 .
 Everything happens on this computer. The language model runs locally with no
 network at all; nothing the child asks or says is sent anywhere. Every
 question passes a word list and the model's own check before it is answered,
 and every answer is checked again. A question that is not suitable gets a
 fixed, kind refusal, and the parent sees it in the panel. The child is told,
 on the same screen, that their family can see some of what they ask:
 BioKids does not do covert monitoring.
 .
 Press-and-speak is a separate switch. The microphone is open only while the
 child holds the button, and the recording is turned into text in memory and
 discarded.
 .
 The models are not in this package. biokids-ai-setup downloads them once,
 when a parent asks, from Hugging Face, checked against pinned checksums; all
 are under the Apache-2.0 or MIT licence. llama.cpp and whisper.cpp are run as
 separate programs.

Package: biokids-archive-keyring
Architecture: all
Multi-Arch: foreign
Section: misc
# This package cannot be built without the real archive key, which exists only
# at release time. CI builds with `-Ppkg.biokids.nokeyring` so that everything
# else is still verified on every push; the release build does not, so a release
# that somehow lost the key fails loudly instead of shipping eight packages and
# no way to trust them.
Build-Profiles: <!pkg.biokids.nokeyring>
Depends: ${misc:Depends}
Description: GnuPG archive key for the BioKids APT repository
 This package ships the public half of the key that signs the BioKids APT
 repository at https://apt.biokids.bilalgurkansanli.com, as
 /usr/share/keyrings/biokids-archive-keyring.gpg.
 .
 Point an apt source at that file with signed-by= and apt will accept
 BioKids packages and nothing else. The key's private half exists only in the
 project's release automation; it has never been on a developer's machine and
 is not in the source repository.
 .
 Installing this package does not add the repository to apt. The two commands
 that do are in the project's README, and they are deliberately something a
 person types rather than something a package does behind their back.
